{
  "case_id": "2007-06-1498",
  "document_hash": "5647d6f2d84d86b1d5aa2274e2e65b79ef173698de2eb4212ab68d47557f3e49",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Carter G. Phillips",
      "case_id": "2007-06-1498",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "5647d6f2d84d86b1d5aa2274e2e65b79ef173698de2eb4212ab68d47557f3e49",
      "evidence_id": "ev-5647d6f2d84d-0000-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Six years ago, this Court in Buckman recognized that policing fraud against Federal agencies is hardly a field the states have traditionally occupied. Based on that premise, this Court in Buckman struck down a novel State tort that was based on the whole concept of fraud on the FDA. And the Court concluded that that tortious analysis as a matter of State law would inevitably conflict with the FDA's responsibility to police fraud."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2007-06-1498",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "5647d6f2d84d86b1d5aa2274e2e65b79ef173698de2eb4212ab68d47557f3e49",
      "evidence_id": "ev-5647d6f2d84d-0000-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "What if the statute didn't have that provision, but it just said you can bring a State tort action when the conditions approved by the FDA for the marketing of this drug have not been complied with? That's all it says. Now, would you knowledge that that -- that that suit could be brought?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "United States",
      "case_id": "2007-06-1498",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "5647d6f2d84d86b1d5aa2274e2e65b79ef173698de2eb4212ab68d47557f3e49",
      "evidence_id": "ev-5647d6f2d84d-0000-0001",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 17,
        "start": 17
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The Michigan statute presents the same conflict this Court found in Buckman, because it requires the determination of fraud on the FDA as a necessary predicate for establishing liability. And as this Court explained in Buckman, the relationship between a Federal agency and the entities it regulates is inherently Federal."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Souter",
      "case_id": "2007-06-1498",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "5647d6f2d84d86b1d5aa2274e2e65b79ef173698de2eb4212ab68d47557f3e49",
      "evidence_id": "ev-5647d6f2d84d-0001-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 20,
        "start": 20
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SOUTER: No, but that's what you've got here, except that the mere admissibility of the evidence turns in part on what the -- the FDA would have done."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Joseffer",
      "case_id": "2007-06-1498",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "5647d6f2d84d86b1d5aa2274e2e65b79ef173698de2eb4212ab68d47557f3e49",
      "evidence_id": "ev-5647d6f2d84d-0001-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 20,
        "start": 20
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. JOSEFFER: Well, if it really is a mere matter of evidence, and that's not what the jury is be asked to find -- and by the way, it's not at all clear that there's -- that there's -- it's settled common law tradition in this type of litigation, because the context here, where a Federal agency does a product-specific approval based in part on a submission of information from a manufacturer, that's not a -- that's a question that, first, is of relatively modern vintage and, second, is not terribly common. So there's not really a uniform, deeply rooted common law tradition here."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Stevens",
      "case_id": "2007-06-1498",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "5647d6f2d84d86b1d5aa2274e2e65b79ef173698de2eb4212ab68d47557f3e49",
      "evidence_id": "ev-5647d6f2d84d-0001-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 22,
        "start": 22
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE STEVENS: May I ask this sort of general question? Apart from Buckman itself, which describes a very serious theoretical problem, as I understand it, there must have been a fair amount of litigation over the years where the regulatory compliance defense was raised or challenged or so forth. Is there -- are there any reported cases describing the magnitude of the problem to the government, when the -- as the result of debate about these issues?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Ms. Zieve",
      "case_id": "2007-06-1498",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "5647d6f2d84d86b1d5aa2274e2e65b79ef173698de2eb4212ab68d47557f3e49",
      "evidence_id": "ev-5647d6f2d84d-0001-0004",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 33,
        "start": 33
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MS. ZIEVE: The Michigan statute takes as its starting point the notion that Federal approval is reliable evidence that a drug company has satisfied the duty -- State law duties of care owed to patients, and then it says: But there are a couple of situations where that reliability is drawn into question."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Alito",
      "case_id": "2007-06-1498",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "5647d6f2d84d86b1d5aa2274e2e65b79ef173698de2eb4212ab68d47557f3e49",
      "evidence_id": "ev-5647d6f2d84d-0001-0005",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 36,
        "start": 36
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE ALITO: Would you explain why you think Mr. Joseffer was wrong when he argued that having a jury decide whether the FDA would have approved the drug or would have withdrawn it from the market if additional or different information had been supplied is incorrect? Doesn't that -- wouldn't that very seriously interfere with what the FDA is doing?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2007-06-1498",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "5647d6f2d84d86b1d5aa2274e2e65b79ef173698de2eb4212ab68d47557f3e49",
      "evidence_id": "ev-5647d6f2d84d-0001-0006",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 37,
        "start": 37
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GINSBURG: Wasn't -- in this case one of the charges was that the original FDA examiner had recommended against approval for this drug, and then something happened inside the FDA, and that examiner was taken off the matter, and another one who approved it was put on? Isn't that the kind of thing that the FDA would want to police itself and not have State courts look into?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Alito",
      "case_id": "2007-06-1498",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "5647d6f2d84d86b1d5aa2274e2e65b79ef173698de2eb4212ab68d47557f3e49",
      "evidence_id": "ev-5647d6f2d84d-0002-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 38,
        "start": 38
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE ALITO: Well, what evidence would you introduce to prove the -- to prove the exception if the Second Circuit's decision stands?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Ms. Zieve",
      "case_id": "2007-06-1498",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "5647d6f2d84d86b1d5aa2274e2e65b79ef173698de2eb4212ab68d47557f3e49",
      "evidence_id": "ev-5647d6f2d84d-0002-0001",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 38,
        "start": 38
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Deposition testimony from that medical officer, for example. There are e-mails. We cited a couple of e-mails in the red brief of things that were stated at the time: One an e-mail to Warner-Lambert and one from a medical officer to his superior talking about the way in which Warner-Lambert made it harder -- to be kind to -- for them to assess what the true safety profile of the drug was."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2007-06-1498",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "5647d6f2d84d86b1d5aa2274e2e65b79ef173698de2eb4212ab68d47557f3e49",
      "evidence_id": "ev-5647d6f2d84d-0002-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 39,
        "start": 39
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GINSBURG: The question is: Would we be disrupting the FDA by taking depositions of examiners to find out what went on at the FDA?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2007-06-1498",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "5647d6f2d84d86b1d5aa2274e2e65b79ef173698de2eb4212ab68d47557f3e49",
      "evidence_id": "ev-5647d6f2d84d-0002-0003",
      "kind": "stated_consequence",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 40,
        "start": 40
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "I assume that if this drug were still on the market, you could bring forward the information that you have alluded to about the withholding of necessary data by Warner-Lambert, and the FDA would certainly be able to consider that and decide whether sanctions were necessary, withdrawing of the drug was necessary."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Breyer",
      "case_id": "2007-06-1498",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "5647d6f2d84d86b1d5aa2274e2e65b79ef173698de2eb4212ab68d47557f3e49",
      "evidence_id": "ev-5647d6f2d84d-0002-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 43,
        "start": 43
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE BREYER: So the the question is: Who is more likely to be right?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kennedy",
      "case_id": "2007-06-1498",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "5647d6f2d84d86b1d5aa2274e2e65b79ef173698de2eb4212ab68d47557f3e49",
      "evidence_id": "ev-5647d6f2d84d-0002-0005",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 46,
        "start": 46
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE KENNEDY: And in your view could a State prohibit introduction of evidence by the defendant that the drug was approved by the FDA?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2007-06-1498",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "5647d6f2d84d86b1d5aa2274e2e65b79ef173698de2eb4212ab68d47557f3e49",
      "evidence_id": "ev-5647d6f2d84d-0002-0006",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 48,
        "start": 48
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SCALIA: What's the Sixth Circuit case? It must have involved this, no?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2007-06-1498",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "5647d6f2d84d86b1d5aa2274e2e65b79ef173698de2eb4212ab68d47557f3e49",
      "evidence_id": "ev-5647d6f2d84d-0002-0007",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 55,
        "start": 55
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The government told us in its brief that the FDA has no system for addressing public complaints -- this was in their brief at page 24 -- because that would divert attention from their primary mission. So there's no action for fraud that one can bring to the FDA."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Source extraction",
      "case_id": "2007-06-1498",
      "confidence": 0.0,
      "document_hash": "5647d6f2d84d86b1d5aa2274e2e65b79ef173698de2eb4212ab68d47557f3e49",
